  |title Repetition helps when the behavior is in your context rather than your training

  |quote
    I'm being repetative because that's sometimes helpful when the behavior is not so much in your training as in your context-- helps to actively apply the newer intellectual understanding when the instinctive token generation is driving.

  |occasion
    Closing a long, deliberately repetitive brief — explaining the repetition instead of
    apologizing for it.

  |about
    A mechanism claim that explains a lot of this estate's apparently redundant
    guidance, including why `AGENTIC-DELEGATION.md` is imported whole rather than
    indexed. Newly-acquired intellectual understanding sits in context; the competing
    behavior sits in weights, and drives generation by default. Repetition is the lever
    that keeps the context-held understanding actively applied against it — so
    redundancy here is a designed countermeasure, not sloppiness, and stripping it is a
    real subtraction.

  |before-acting
    - You are about to compress or de-duplicate guidance because it repeats itself.
    - You notice you understood something earlier in the session but are not applying it
      now.
    - You are writing durable guidance against a strongly-trained default.
    - You are trimming a document for concision.

  |see
    - #exm-compliance-is-not-persuasion

